Vibe‑Kanban Usage Rules

Purpose
Provide a consistent workflow for creating, updating, reviewing, and completing tickets with the Vibe‑Kanban MCP. Keep ticket metadata actionable and auditable.

Ticket Structure
- Title: Short, action-oriented summary.
- Description: Contains:
  - Context/goal
  - Clear instructions/checklist
  - References (files, commands, docs)
  - Execution Summary section appended during/after work (commands run, files changed, artifacts, counts/results)
- Status: one of `todo`, `in-progress`, `in-review`, `done`, `cancelled`.

Status Semantics
- `todo`: Not started; ready for pickup.
- `in-progress`: Work started; updates should appear in Execution Summary.
- `in-review`: Implementation completed; waiting for review/validation.
- `done`: Reviewed and accepted; artifacts/results confirmed.
- `cancelled`: No longer applicable.

Standard Actions
- Start work: set status `in-progress` and record start context in the description.
- Append updates: add an “Execution Summary (YYYY-MM-DD)” block; list commands run, files modified/added, artifacts produced, and key counts.
- Request review: move to `in-review` and ensure summary is up to date.
- Complete: after validation, set to `done` and add a final one-line acceptance note at the end of the summary.
- Reopen: if issues found during review, set back to `todo` or `in-progress` and add a short reason at the end of the last summary block.

Execution Summary Template
---
Execution Summary (YYYY-MM-DD)
- Commands: list exact commands used
- Code: files edited/added (paths)
- Artifacts: output files (paths)
- Results: key counts/IDs/status updates
- Next: follow-ups or pointers for reviewers
---

MCP Commands (typical)
- Update description/status: use the MCP ticket update to set `description` and/or `status`.
- Add attempt: start a task attempt on the active branch for traceability.

Good Practices
- Reference files and commands with absolute or workspace-rooted paths.
- Prefer appending to description over replacing; keep a chronological log via Execution Summary blocks.
- Do not manually create follow-up tickets unless explicitly asked; rely on batchers/right-time emission where configured.

Security & Hygiene
- Do not include secrets in tickets.
- Link to local artifacts and databases; avoid pasting large data dumps.

